<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chuvash people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chuvash are a Turkic people of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, most of whom live in the Chuvash Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with its capital at Cheboksary. Their language is the only living member of the Oghur branch of Turkic, and they are widely seen as descendants of the Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>. Unlike their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> neighbours, most were converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a> under Russian rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chuvash-people.jpg" alt="Chuvash people" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zelenov Ivan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Chuvash_men.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Chuvash</b>, spoken today in the Volga region of Russia, is the only living language of the Oghuric branch of Turkic, the branch spoken by the Volga Bulgars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In late 1917 the National Assembly of Muslims of Inner Russia and Siberia resolved to create the Idel-Ural State, uniting Tatars, Bashkirs and <b>Chuvash</b>, with Kazan as capital, and its proclamation was planned for March 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/idel-ural-state">Idel-Ural State</a></li><li><blockquote>Kazan ruled Tatars, <b>Chuvash</b>, Mari, Udmurts and Bashkirs, with a farming and trading economy linked to the Volga route.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a></li></ul></section>
